WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.240
- [James] Thanks to INDOCHINO

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:04.513
for keeping Legal Eagle in the
air, and helping me look fly.

00:00:05.861 --> 00:00:07.760
- Rights, I'm sick to death
of hearing about rights!

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.848
What about my right not
to have my life work

00:00:09.848 --> 00:00:12.332
subverted by blind ignorance?

00:00:12.332 --> 00:00:14.130
- That's not an actual right.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.713
(upbeat music)

00:00:18.679 --> 00:00:21.271
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer.

00:00:21.271 --> 00:00:22.858
Today, we are covering an episode of

00:00:22.858 --> 00:00:24.311
Star Trek: The Next Generation,

00:00:24.311 --> 00:00:25.858
it's called Measure of A Man.

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:27.779
It's the episode where
Data gets put on trial

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:30.420
to determine whether he
is a man or a machine,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.086
or both, I guess.

00:00:32.086 --> 00:00:33.350
It raises all kinds of

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.358
great philosophical and legal questions:

00:00:35.358 --> 00:00:36.724
are you a person?

00:00:36.724 --> 00:00:40.007
Do you have rights under
the US Constitution?

00:00:40.007 --> 00:00:42.366
And it tells us that
even in the 24th century

00:00:42.366 --> 00:00:44.825
where you have warp drive and teleporters,

00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:47.434
you're still gonna need lawyers.

00:00:47.434 --> 00:00:49.351
- Da da da da da da da.

00:00:51.756 --> 00:00:53.550
- As always, be sure to
hit that bell and comment

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.046
in the form of an objection,

00:00:55.046 --> 00:00:57.471
which I'll either sustain or overrule.

00:00:57.471 --> 00:01:00.041
And of course, stick around
until the end of the video

00:01:00.041 --> 00:01:01.899
where I give Star Trek:
The Next Generation

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:05.352
Measure of A Man a
grade for legal realism.

00:01:05.352 --> 00:01:08.521
So, without further ado, let's
dig in to Measure of A Man.

00:01:08.521 --> 00:01:11.718
- Explain this procedure. (clears throat)

00:01:11.718 --> 00:01:13.563
- Ever since I first saw Data

00:01:13.563 --> 00:01:15.638
at the entrance evaluation
at the Starfleet Academy,

00:01:15.638 --> 00:01:17.722
I wanted to understand it.

00:01:17.722 --> 00:01:20.314
I became a student of the works of

00:01:20.314 --> 00:01:22.660
Dr. Noonien Soong, Data's creator,

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.724
and I've tried to continue his work.

00:01:25.724 --> 00:01:28.954
I believe I am very close to
the breakthrough that will

00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:31.681
enable me to duplicate Dr. Soong's work,

00:01:31.681 --> 00:01:33.264
and replicate this.

00:01:37.975 --> 00:01:42.951
But as a first step, I must
disassemble and study it.

00:01:42.951 --> 00:01:45.368
Data is going to be my guide.

00:01:46.285 --> 00:01:49.218
- So, the crux of this episode
is that Data is an android.

00:01:49.218 --> 00:01:51.453
He is an artificial form of life.

00:01:51.453 --> 00:01:53.682
A member of Starfleet Medical wants to

00:01:53.682 --> 00:01:56.550
conduct scientific experiments on Data

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.485
in the hopes of being able
to replicate the intelligence

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:02.202
that forms Data's positronic brain.

00:02:02.202 --> 00:02:04.071
- You've constructed a positronic brain?

00:02:04.071 --> 00:02:04.904
- Yes.

00:02:05.966 --> 00:02:07.558
- Data doesn't want to do that.

00:02:07.558 --> 00:02:10.253
He thinks that the Starfleet
officer doesn't have the

00:02:10.253 --> 00:02:13.805
ability to conduct these
experiments safely,

00:02:13.805 --> 00:02:15.592
and Captain Picard isn't willing

00:02:15.592 --> 00:02:17.184
to give up a member of his crew

00:02:17.184 --> 00:02:19.225
to be subjected to these experiments.

00:02:19.225 --> 00:02:21.355
So the question is, does
Data have the autonomy

00:02:21.355 --> 00:02:24.870
as a person or a thing that
is endowed with personhood

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.409
to be able to reject this request?

00:02:27.409 --> 00:02:29.329
Or is he a thing that is subject

00:02:29.329 --> 00:02:31.774
to the whims of Starfleet,

00:02:31.774 --> 00:02:34.833
like some computer or some machine?

00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:36.795
I think this episode is
meant to harken back to

00:02:36.795 --> 00:02:38.778
some of the darker periods of US history

00:02:38.778 --> 00:02:40.556
because the US Military itself

00:02:40.556 --> 00:02:42.961
doesn't have a great track record

00:02:42.961 --> 00:02:45.699
when it comes to subjecting
members of the Military

00:02:45.699 --> 00:02:47.247
and sometimes members of the public

00:02:47.247 --> 00:02:49.768
to illegal experimentation.

00:02:49.768 --> 00:02:51.996
You can look at the Tuskegee experiments

00:02:51.996 --> 00:02:53.135
in World War 2,

00:02:53.135 --> 00:02:56.114
you can look at the MKUltra
experiments in the 60s and 70s.

00:02:56.114 --> 00:02:58.338
Unfortunately, some
people weren't even asked

00:02:58.338 --> 00:03:00.753
to be subjected to these
medical experiments,

00:03:00.753 --> 00:03:04.138
so at least Data has the
opportunity to defend himself

00:03:04.138 --> 00:03:05.327
and the right to a trial.

00:03:05.327 --> 00:03:07.421
- Your response is
emotional and irrational.

00:03:07.421 --> 00:03:08.421
- Irrational?

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:10.041
- You are endowing Data
with human characteristics

00:03:10.041 --> 00:03:12.416
because it looks human, but it is not.

00:03:12.416 --> 00:03:14.543
If it were a box on wheels,

00:03:14.543 --> 00:03:16.629
I would not be facing this opposition.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.298
- Overt sentimentality is not

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:20.168
one of Captain Picard's failings-

00:03:20.168 --> 00:03:21.859
- Boom.
- Trust me, I know.

00:03:21.859 --> 00:03:22.860
- Oh, snap!

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.263
- I will tell you again,

00:03:24.263 --> 00:03:27.216
Data is a valued member of my crew,

00:03:27.216 --> 00:03:29.149
he is an outstanding bridge officer.

00:03:29.149 --> 00:03:31.532
- If I am permitted to
make this experiment,

00:03:31.532 --> 00:03:35.184
the horizons for human
achievements become boundless.

00:03:35.184 --> 00:03:37.301
- Frankly, I think that these questions

00:03:37.301 --> 00:03:39.607
probably would have been
solved the moment that

00:03:39.607 --> 00:03:41.386
Data entered into Starfleet.

00:03:41.386 --> 00:03:42.939
You wouldn't let a box on wheels

00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:44.865
enter into Starfleet Academy

00:03:44.865 --> 00:03:47.492
and go through all of the
different academic training

00:03:47.492 --> 00:03:49.204
that is required into Starfleet.

00:03:49.204 --> 00:03:52.344
So realistically speaking, I
think some of these questions

00:03:52.344 --> 00:03:53.828
would've already been solved

00:03:53.828 --> 00:03:56.674
but it's interesting to
tackle them now, at least.

00:03:56.674 --> 00:04:01.025
- Consider every ship in
Starfleet with a Data on board,

00:04:01.025 --> 00:04:04.129
utilizing its extraordinary capabilities,

00:04:04.129 --> 00:04:07.565
acting as our hands and eyes
in dangerous situations.

00:04:07.565 --> 00:04:08.968
- Look, you're preaching
to the choir here,

00:04:08.968 --> 00:04:10.879
why don't you get to the point?

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:14.754
- It's a classic ends justify
the means argument there.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:17.435
- Data must not be permitted to resign.

00:04:17.435 --> 00:04:20.538
- Data is a Starfleet officer,
he still has certain rights.

00:04:20.538 --> 00:04:23.273
- Rights, rights, I'm sick to
death of hearing about rights!

00:04:23.273 --> 00:04:25.397
What about my right not
to have my life work

00:04:25.397 --> 00:04:27.893
subverted by blind ignorance?

00:04:27.893 --> 00:04:29.579
- That's, that's not an actual right.

00:04:29.579 --> 00:04:32.420
- We have rule of law in this federation,

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:35.130
you cannot simply seize people
and experiment with them

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:36.590
to prove your pet theories.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:37.423
- Thank you.

00:04:37.423 --> 00:04:38.380
- [Maddox] Now you are doing it.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:40.779
- Yeah, generally speaking,
that would be right.

00:04:40.779 --> 00:04:43.580
- Data is an extraordinary
piece of engineering,

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:44.834
but it is a machine!

00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:46.586
If you permit it to resign,

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:49.235
it will destroy years of work in robotics.

00:04:49.235 --> 00:04:53.090
Starfleet does not have
to allow the resignation.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.490
- Commander, who do you
think you're working for?

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:57.306
Starfleet is not an
organization that ignores

00:04:57.306 --> 00:05:00.365
its own regulations when
they become inconvenient.

00:05:00.365 --> 00:05:03.035
Whether you like it or
not, Data does have rights.

00:05:03.035 --> 00:05:06.450
- That is sort of the
rule of law in a nutshell.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.259
The point is that the laws we have

00:05:08.259 --> 00:05:11.870
should be universal and
applied to everyone.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:13.803
They should not be changed on a whim

00:05:13.803 --> 00:05:16.410
and given ad hoc exemptions based on

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:18.721
philosophical justifications

00:05:18.721 --> 00:05:20.820
like the ends justifying the means.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.500
A lot of people talk about
the first amendment right

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:24.726
to freedom of speech

00:05:24.726 --> 00:05:27.410
but at the same time, they
complain when they hear

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:30.790
people use speech in ways
that they don't like,

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:33.130
but the point is that
it's a universal right

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:36.410
and that maybe there would
be more beneficial outcomes.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:39.768
But the point is that individual
rights cannot be subverted

00:05:39.768 --> 00:05:44.020
based on whim or based on circumstance,

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.280
they're supposed to be universal.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:47.615
- Let me put it another way.

00:05:47.615 --> 00:05:51.012
Would you permit the
computer of the enterprise

00:05:51.012 --> 00:05:53.193
to refuse a refit?

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.290
- That's an interesting point

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:00.077
but the enterprise computer is property.

00:06:00.077 --> 00:06:00.930
Is Data?

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:01.763
- Of course.

00:06:01.763 --> 00:06:05.442
- (sighs) There may be a law
to support this position.

00:06:05.442 --> 00:06:07.540
- Then find it.

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:10.070
A ruling with such broad
ranging implications

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:11.600
must be supported.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.764
- Yeah, so that would
be generally correct.

00:06:13.764 --> 00:06:16.860
A computer is moveable property,

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:18.750
in other words, it's what we call chattel.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:20.486
In fact, in my analysis of

00:06:20.486 --> 00:06:23.015
the Sokovia Accords in
the Marvel Universe,

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:27.170
I had an offhand joked is
Vision is really just a computer

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:32.170
and therefore Vision was
chattel to Tony Stark.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:33.427
- Data is a toaster.

00:06:33.427 --> 00:06:35.347
- I don't think it's that simple.

00:06:35.347 --> 00:06:37.660
- But the same arguments could apply here

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:39.160
and the odds are there's not going to be

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.290
specific legislation out there that says

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:43.440
a computer of specific intelligence

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.498
is considered a person with respect to

00:06:46.498 --> 00:06:48.650
all of the constitutional rights

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:51.560
that are endowed to actual human people.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:54.600
But that is sort of the point
of some of this case law

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.050
is that when there is a
new example out there,

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.870
you have to actually go through a trial

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:01.850
to determine what the rights are.

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:03.470
- I have completed my research.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:05.010
Based on the Acts of Cumberland

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:06.814
passed in the early 21st century,

00:07:06.814 --> 00:07:09.053
Data is the property of Starfleet.

00:07:09.053 --> 00:07:10.536
He cannot resign

00:07:10.536 --> 00:07:13.316
and he cannot refuse to
cooperate with Commander Maddox.

00:07:13.316 --> 00:07:16.973
- I would very much like to
see the language that is used

00:07:16.973 --> 00:07:20.610
in the Acts of Cumberland
that she's referring to.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:22.270
I guess maybe I have to stand corrected

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:25.190
on my prior statement about
there being legislation

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:28.880
that deals with whether a
computer is sufficiently human

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:31.970
to constitute a person
under the Constitution.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:34.220
But that being said, this
is actually reminiscent

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:38.220
of the Dred Scott v.
Sandford decision in 1857.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:40.159
That was the landmark
Supreme Court decision

00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:44.016
that held that the Constitution
did not include citizenship

00:07:44.016 --> 00:07:46.410
for black Americans,

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:49.520
regardless of whether they
were enslaved or free,

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:51.019
and therefore the rights and privileges

00:07:51.019 --> 00:07:53.455
that citizenship confers
on American citizens

00:07:53.455 --> 00:07:56.270
would never apply to black Americans.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:59.100
The plaintiff in that case
was a man named Dred Scott,

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:01.140
who was a slave at the time

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:02.920
and whose owners took him from Missouri,

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:04.540
which was a slave state,

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:06.238
into the Missouri Territory,

00:08:06.238 --> 00:08:09.430
most of which had been designated as free

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:11.940
by the Missouri Compromise in 1820.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:15.290
When Scott's owners took
him back to Missouri,

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.280
he sued in court, claiming that

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.851
because he had been taken
into a free US territory,

00:08:19.851 --> 00:08:21.932
he had automatically been freed,

00:08:21.932 --> 00:08:24.500
been conferred with the
rights of an American citizen

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.830
and was no longer a slave.

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:28.750
In a decision that remained a blight

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:31.771
on the record of the US
Supreme Court for years,

00:08:31.771 --> 00:08:34.070
they ruled that the Constitution

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:36.800
simply did not apply to African Americans.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.320
So in essence, Data is going to

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.450
go through a similar trial here.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:41.786
- What if I challenge this ruling?

00:08:41.786 --> 00:08:44.325
- Then I shall be required
to hold a hearing.

00:08:44.325 --> 00:08:46.940
- Then I so challenge,
convene your hearing.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:48.608
- Convene your hearing!

00:08:48.608 --> 00:08:50.938
- Captain, that would be
exceedingly difficult.

00:08:50.938 --> 00:08:54.203
This is a new base, I have no staff.

00:08:54.203 --> 00:08:57.571
- But surely, Captain,
you have regulations

00:08:57.571 --> 00:09:00.720
to take care of such an eventuality.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:01.757
- Or just make new law.

00:09:01.757 --> 00:09:04.685
- I can use serving
officers as legal counsel.

00:09:04.685 --> 00:09:07.313
You, as the senior officer, would defend.

00:09:08.415 --> 00:09:09.415
- Very good.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:13.380
- And the unenviable task
of prosecuting this case

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:14.580
would fall on you, Commander,

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:17.380
as the next most senior officer
of the defendant's ship.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.641
- I can't, I won't.

00:09:21.641 --> 00:09:24.430
Data's my comrade, we
have served together.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:27.108
I not only respect him,
I consider him my friend.

00:09:27.108 --> 00:09:30.742
- When people of good conscience
have an honest dispute,

00:09:30.742 --> 00:09:33.120
we must still sometimes resort to

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.743
this kind of adversarial system.

00:09:35.743 --> 00:09:38.360
- You just want me to prove
that Data is a mere machine.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.169
I can't do that because
I don't believe it.

00:09:40.169 --> 00:09:41.550
I happen to know better,

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:43.830
so I'm neither qualified nor willing.

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:46.560
You're going to have to find someone else.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.820
- Then I will rule summarily
based on my findings.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:52.680
- Yeah, so that doesn't work
for a couple of reasons.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:55.682
Number one, a lawyer has
to be a zealous advocate

00:09:55.682 --> 00:09:58.000
for his or her client.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.374
So if you strongly believe that

00:10:00.374 --> 00:10:04.250
you cannot put forth a good defense

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:05.428
or a good prosecution,

00:10:05.428 --> 00:10:08.323
ethically, I think you are
unable to fulfill the task

00:10:08.323 --> 00:10:10.370
and I think you need to step aside.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:12.860
Additionally, it would
be completely improper

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:15.410
for the judge to rule on an ad hoc basis

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:18.760
that just because a lawyer
refuses to prosecute the case

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:22.380
to then rule against the defense,

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:24.150
that would be a huge violation of

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:25.730
the rights of the defendant

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.630
which, I think you have to assume that

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:29.450
Data gets the benefit of the doubt

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:30.950
at least in the first instance

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:33.120
that he is a person, a member of Starfleet

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:34.670
and entitled to certain rights.

00:10:38.743 --> 00:10:42.440
- [Machine] Verified,
Lieutenant Commander Data.

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:43.790
- So I should point out
that as a defendant,

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:45.697
you always wanna put
your best foot forward

00:10:45.697 --> 00:10:49.320
and wearing a yellow one
piece bodysuit to court

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:52.540
is not the best way to dress for court.

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:53.610
(whispers) INDOCHINO.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:55.510
- Who built you, Commander?

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:56.343
- Dr. Noonien Soong.

00:10:56.343 --> 00:10:57.820
- [Riker] And he was?

00:10:57.820 --> 00:10:59.871
- The foremost authority on cybernetics.

00:10:59.871 --> 00:11:02.743
- More basic than that, what was he?

00:11:06.055 --> 00:11:06.888
- Human?

00:11:09.282 --> 00:11:10.115
- Thank you.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.850
Commander, what is the
capacity of your memory

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:16.330
and how fast can you access information?

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:18.709
- I have an ultimate storage capacity of

00:11:18.709 --> 00:11:20.574
800 quadrillion bits,

00:11:20.574 --> 00:11:23.190
my total linear computational speed

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:26.153
has been rated at 60 trillion
operations per second.

00:11:31.216 --> 00:11:34.960
- Your Honor, I offer in
evidence prosecution's exhibit A,

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:38.890
a rod of par-steel, tensile
strength, 40 kilobars.

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:40.830
- All right, that's a
pretty reasonable way

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.472
to add something into evidence.

00:11:43.472 --> 00:11:45.972
I think he probably should
have laid some more foundation

00:11:45.972 --> 00:11:48.953
since he's not an expert in types of steel

00:11:48.953 --> 00:11:51.750
or the tensile strength of steel,

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:53.930
but at least he entered it into evidence

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:55.620
before having the witness talk about it,

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:56.850
which is actually something that

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:59.610
a lot of legal dramas
actually forget to do.

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:01.610
So that part was reasonably accurate.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:03.456
Of course, the judge forgot to rule

00:12:03.456 --> 00:12:07.409
on the motion to add that
piece of steel into evidence.

00:12:07.409 --> 00:12:09.643
In court, you actually have to say

00:12:09.643 --> 00:12:11.070
the thing that you're doing,

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:13.090
you can't just nod
because the stenographer

00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:15.630
can't actually take down nods so,

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:17.120
the judge sort of said, yes,

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:19.981
motion to enter the bar of
steel into evidence is granted,

00:12:19.981 --> 00:12:23.250
so that it is actually
reflected in the record.

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:24.630
- Commander, would you bend that?

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:25.463
- Objection!

00:12:25.463 --> 00:12:28.337
There are many life forms
possessed with mega strength.

00:12:28.337 --> 00:12:30.950
These issues are not
relevant to this hearing.

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:32.910
- I'm afraid I can't agree, Captain.

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:35.134
Proceed with your
demonstration, Commander.

00:12:35.134 --> 00:12:37.570
- Yeah, that's not a great objection

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:40.010
and it was a good ruling
by the judge here.

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:41.820
Essentially what Captain Picard was doing

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:45.691
is saying that it's not going
to prove the ultimate issue,

00:12:45.691 --> 00:12:47.696
but his objection is going to

00:12:47.696 --> 00:12:50.010
the weight of the particular evidence

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:51.760
not the admissibility.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:53.820
You can enter things into evidence

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:56.400
even if they have only
a tenuous relation to

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.970
the ultimate issue,

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:02.040
in this case, whether
Data is human or not.

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:04.900
It helps prove or disprove the idea that

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:06.730
he is human or property.

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:09.370
So Captain Picard was wrong to object

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:13.462
just because it's not as
persuasive as Riker thinks it is,

00:13:13.462 --> 00:13:15.740
so that it should not be
entered into evidence.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:18.388
That is a question for the
trier of fact to decide

00:13:18.388 --> 00:13:21.330
and that's why the judge
overruled his objection,

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:23.180
because he goes to the
weight not the admissibility

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:25.220
of that particular piece of evidence.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:26.870
- Drawing on the log of the construction

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:28.530
of the prototype android, Lore,

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:30.513
also constructed by Noonien Soong,

00:13:30.513 --> 00:13:35.411
I request to be allowed to
remove the Commander's hand

00:13:35.411 --> 00:13:36.930
for your inspection.

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:37.763
- Objection.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:42.530
- You should probably state your grounds.

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:43.380
- Doesn't matter.

00:13:46.284 --> 00:13:47.990
Objection withdrawn.

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:50.713
- Okay, not sure what the
objection would've been.

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:52.980
- Proceed, Commander.

00:13:57.711 --> 00:13:58.973
I'm sorry.

00:14:00.234 --> 00:14:02.651
(dark music)

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:22.080
The Commander is a physical
representation of a dream,

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:26.070
an idea conceived of by the mind of a man.

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:29.070
- Here, Riker is just
lapsing into argument

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:30.070
which you're not allowed to do

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:33.210
while you're in the middle
of examining a witness.

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:34.979
There are examples where you can conduct

00:14:34.979 --> 00:14:37.680
a physical examination of a witness,

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:39.620
which is effectively what Riker has done.

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:41.720
You might recall that
in the OJ Simpson trial,

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:44.650
there was a big deal made
about OJ Simpson's ability

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:47.448
or inability to wear
a glove that was found

00:14:47.448 --> 00:14:49.980
at the scene of the crime.

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:52.600
- If it doesn't fit, you must acquit.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:55.020
- And effectively, Riker has
done something similar here

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:57.610
in terms of removing Data's hand.

00:14:57.610 --> 00:14:59.210
You generally don't wanna do that

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.138
because you don't know
exactly how it's going to go

00:15:01.138 --> 00:15:05.040
but here, it's fairly safe
because Riker knew that

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:07.564
he could remove Data's
hand without much fuss

00:15:07.564 --> 00:15:09.410
and that's exactly what he did.

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:12.480
The problem is that he is
lapsing straight into argument,

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:13.894
effectively his closing argument,

00:15:13.894 --> 00:15:16.517
while in the middle of
asking Data questions

00:15:16.517 --> 00:15:18.540
and doing a physical examination.

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:19.990
That you are not allowed to do

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:22.418
and Picard should be objecting here.

00:15:22.418 --> 00:15:26.589
- Its purpose, to serve to
human needs and interests.

00:15:26.589 --> 00:15:27.422
- Arguments.

00:15:27.422 --> 00:15:29.210
- A collection of neural nets-

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:30.043
- Ask a question!

00:15:30.043 --> 00:15:32.127
- Heuristic algorithms.

00:15:32.127 --> 00:15:36.252
Its responses dictated
by an elaborate software

00:15:36.252 --> 00:15:41.070
written by a man, its
hardware built by a man,

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:45.751
and now, and now a man will shut it off.

00:15:45.751 --> 00:15:48.635
- So, the point is well taken that

00:15:48.635 --> 00:15:52.430
shutting off Data means
he's not actual human life.

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:55.120
Although you could argue that
Riker just committed murder

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.330
because he turned Data off.

00:15:57.330 --> 00:15:59.910
The bigger problem from a trial
lawyer perspective is that

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:01.780
he is engaging in argument

00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:04.510
while he should be questioning Data.

00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:05.750
- Pinocchio is broken.

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:06.583
- You're broken.

00:16:06.583 --> 00:16:07.600
- Its strings have been cut.

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:09.244
- More argument.

00:16:09.244 --> 00:16:11.455
- Commander Riker has
dramatically demonstrated

00:16:11.455 --> 00:16:16.455
to this court that Lieutenant
Commander Data is a machine.

00:16:16.536 --> 00:16:18.190
Do we deny that?

00:16:18.190 --> 00:16:20.416
No, because it is not relevant.

00:16:20.416 --> 00:16:25.416
We too are machines, just
machines of a different type.

00:16:25.799 --> 00:16:30.115
Commander Riker has also reminded us that

00:16:30.115 --> 00:16:33.590
Lieutenant Commander Data
was created by human,

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:34.750
do we deny that?

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:35.720
No.

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:36.953
Again, it is not relevant.

00:16:36.953 --> 00:16:38.930
- So this is good, despite the fact that

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:40.630
this is total argument that

00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:42.940
you would normally save
for a closing argument

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:44.210
or a motion before the court,

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:47.100
I like what Captain Picard is doing here

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:50.236
which is that he is
admitting to the things that

00:16:50.236 --> 00:16:53.665
are sort of optically bad for his case,

00:16:53.665 --> 00:16:55.200
that Data is a machine,

00:16:55.200 --> 00:16:57.496
that Data was created by another person.

00:16:57.496 --> 00:16:59.320
One of the things that you wanna do,

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:01.516
whether it is a bench
trial or a jury trial,

00:17:01.516 --> 00:17:05.500
is limit the number of issues
you actually have to dispute.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:08.350
So that is a great
tactic by Captain Picard,

00:17:08.350 --> 00:17:10.458
and actually one of the
rhetorical devices that I use

00:17:10.458 --> 00:17:12.580
in my motions on occasion is

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:15.420
I will state what the
opposing side has argued

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:18.994
and I will say, that is as
true as it is irrelevant.

00:17:18.994 --> 00:17:21.520
- I call to the stand
Commander Bruce Maddox

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:22.570
as a hostile witness.

00:17:23.650 --> 00:17:25.490
- So, you don't actually get to call

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:28.970
certain witnesses to the
stand as hostile witnesses.

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:30.370
A hostile witness is a real thing,

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:32.580
that is not just something you see on TV.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:34.982
What that means is that
you have called that person

00:17:34.982 --> 00:17:36.770
as a witness to the stand.

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:38.946
So essentially, you are asking them to

00:17:38.946 --> 00:17:41.625
be subject to a direct examination,

00:17:41.625 --> 00:17:44.102
and as you may have heard in TV,

00:17:44.102 --> 00:17:46.780
you're not allowed to
ask leading questions

00:17:46.780 --> 00:17:50.080
of a witness that you
have called to the stand.

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:53.310
Leading questions are totally
okay for cross examination,

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:55.550
but they're not okay
for direct examination.

00:17:55.550 --> 00:17:57.830
So you are asking the
judge to recognize that

00:17:57.830 --> 00:17:59.033
although it's direct examination,

00:17:59.033 --> 00:18:01.220
the witness is quote hostile

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:03.214
and therefore, you should be able to ask

00:18:03.214 --> 00:18:06.330
leading questions on direct examination.

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:08.140
But you don't get to just
call them to the stand

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:09.350
and say that they're hostile.

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:11.470
- Is your contention that
Lieutenant Commander Data

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:14.150
is not a sentient being
and thereby not entitled

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:16.620
to all the rights reserved
for all life forms

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:18.965
within this federation?

00:18:18.965 --> 00:18:20.853
- Data is not sentient, no.

00:18:20.853 --> 00:18:22.790
Your Honor, would you enlighten us?

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:25.026
What is required for sentience?

00:18:25.026 --> 00:18:30.026
- Intelligence,
self-awareness, consciousness.

00:18:31.046 --> 00:18:33.573
- Prove to the court that I am sentient.

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:36.796
- This is absurd, we all
know you're sentient.

00:18:36.796 --> 00:18:40.020
- So I am sentient, but
Commander Data is not?

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:41.820
- [Maddox] That's right.

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:43.428
- Why?

00:18:43.428 --> 00:18:44.463
Why am I sentient?

00:18:45.877 --> 00:18:47.580
- Well, you are self-aware.

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:49.450
- Ah, that's the second of your criteria.

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:52.142
Let's deal with the first, intelligence.

00:18:52.142 --> 00:18:57.142
- So as a tangent here,
this is a huge question

00:18:57.540 --> 00:18:59.010
not only in the law,

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:00.810
which doesn't really answer the question,

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:03.460
but in philosophy, in neurology.

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:07.410
There are tons of different
definitions of sentience,

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:09.093
of intelligence, of consciousness,

00:19:09.093 --> 00:19:11.048
of the theory of the mind,

00:19:11.048 --> 00:19:14.230
and there's really no good way

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:16.250
of being able to prove that

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:18.670
someone or something is sentient.

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:21.420
But with any definition that
you're going to run across,

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:23.291
you're going to have problems
of over-inclusiveness

00:19:23.291 --> 00:19:25.090
and under-inclusiveness.

00:19:25.090 --> 00:19:26.830
So it's hard not to
feel bad for Maddox here

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:28.810
because it is one of the hardest questions

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:31.940
in not only philosophy,
but neurology as well.

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:33.970
- Is Commander Data intelligent?

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:34.803
- Yes.

00:19:34.803 --> 00:19:37.000
It has the ability to learn and understand

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:40.442
and to cope with new situations.

00:19:40.442 --> 00:19:42.120
- Like this hearing.

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:42.953
- [Maddox] Yes.

00:19:42.953 --> 00:19:45.339
- What about self-awareness?

00:19:45.339 --> 00:19:46.778
What does that mean?

00:19:46.778 --> 00:19:49.671
Why am I self-aware?

00:19:49.671 --> 00:19:53.437
- Because you're conscious of
your existence and actions.

00:19:53.437 --> 00:19:56.772
You are aware of yourself
and your own ego.

00:19:56.772 --> 00:19:59.387
- Commander Data, what are you doing now?

00:19:59.387 --> 00:20:01.910
- I'm taking part in a legal hearing

00:20:01.910 --> 00:20:03.630
to determine my rights and status.

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:05.353
Am I a person or property?

00:20:05.353 --> 00:20:06.726
- And what's at stake?

00:20:06.726 --> 00:20:08.247
- My right to choose.

00:20:08.247 --> 00:20:10.960
Perhaps my very life.

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:15.640
- So, rhetorically it's
great to turn to Data

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:18.613
and ask him a question
that totally destroys

00:20:18.613 --> 00:20:20.700
the statement that the witness just made.

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:23.950
But you are not allowed to ask
questions of another person

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:26.130
while you're in the middle
of a direct examination

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:28.180
of another witness, it
doesn't work that way,

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:30.360
regardless of how dramatic it seems.

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:31.890
It's totally improper.

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:34.730
Now sooner or later, this
man or others like him

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:36.790
will succeed in
replicating Commander Data,

00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:39.210
for the decision you reach here today

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:41.030
will determine how we will regard

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:44.069
this creation of our genius.

00:20:44.069 --> 00:20:46.635
It will reveal the kind
of a people we are,

00:20:46.635 --> 00:20:48.500
what he is destined to be,

00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:50.200
it will reach far beyond this courtroom

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:53.720
and this one android.

00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:54.980
- Generally, you're not supposed to

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:56.540
make these kind of arguments.

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:59.520
This is sort of like Captain
Picard's closing argument here.

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:02.510
And you're meant to adjudicate

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:04.960
the facts of the case before you.

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:07.760
And generally speaking,
the judge or the jury

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:10.684
or the trier of fact is not meant to

00:21:10.684 --> 00:21:14.760
consider the far-reaching implications.

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:18.880
You are simply supposed to
apply the facts to the law

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:19.870
of the case at hand,

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:22.880
and that's sorta the doctrine
of judicial minimalism,

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:25.340
that you don't want people
making broad pronouncements,

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:26.780
you want them to adjudicate just

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:29.920
the smallest thing they possibly can

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:32.110
based on the case that is before them.

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:35.154
- It could significantly
redefine the boundaries

00:21:35.154 --> 00:21:36.960
of personal liberty and freedom.

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:38.563
Expanding them for some,

00:21:38.563 --> 00:21:42.013
savagely curtailing them for others.

00:21:44.279 --> 00:21:45.950
Are you prepared to condemn him

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:47.170
and all who come after him

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:48.503
to servitude and slavery?

00:21:50.036 --> 00:21:52.590
Your Honor, Starfleet was founded

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:54.190
to seek out new life.

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:55.566
- And new civilizations!

00:21:55.566 --> 00:21:59.243
To boldly go where no
lawyer has gone before!

00:21:59.243 --> 00:22:01.153
- Well there it sits!

00:22:03.737 --> 00:22:05.263
Waiting.

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:12.970
You wanted a chance to
make law, well here it is.

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:14.910
Make it a good one.

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:17.250
- All right, and Starfleet decides that

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:20.388
Data is, in fact, a person
and has rights under

00:22:20.388 --> 00:22:23.820
whatever the equivalent of
the Starfleet Constitution is.

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:25.166
- Does Data have a soul?

00:22:25.166 --> 00:22:27.358
I don't know that he has.

00:22:27.358 --> 00:22:29.550
But I've got to give him the freedom

00:22:29.550 --> 00:22:32.313
to explore that question himself.

00:22:32.313 --> 00:22:35.608
(upbeat music)

00:22:35.608 --> 00:22:37.910
- But how realistic was it?

00:22:37.910 --> 00:22:40.610
Well, now it's time to give
Star Trek: The Next Generation,

00:22:40.610 --> 00:22:44.127
the Measure of a Man episode
a grade for legal realism.

00:22:44.127 --> 00:22:45.580
(hammer drops)

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:47.780
On the one hand, you have, obviously,

00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:49.562
a lot of dramatic license taken.

00:22:49.562 --> 00:22:52.210
They are trying to
decide these heady issues

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:54.670
from a member of the
Judge Advocate General

00:22:54.670 --> 00:22:56.340
that's trying to make new law

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:58.760
in a way that sort of a kangaroo court

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:00.660
condensed for 40 minutes on TV.

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:03.470
On the other hand, you have
some reasonable things going on.

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:06.132
You've got someone who is
treated as a hostile witness.

00:23:06.132 --> 00:23:08.530
You have reasonable arguments being made,

00:23:08.530 --> 00:23:12.230
a couple good objections and
some good rulings by the court.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:14.878
So I give this episode of
Star Trek: The Next Generation

00:23:14.878 --> 00:23:16.635
a C+.

00:23:16.635 --> 00:23:20.040
They boldly went where
they were supposed to go.

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:22.090
- Good try, nine out of ten for effort.

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:23.570
- Now, if Data was my client,

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:25.150
the first thing that
I would have him do is

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:27.234
get a proper suit to wear to court.

00:23:27.234 --> 00:23:29.970
Frankly, a yellow one piece bodysuit

00:23:29.970 --> 00:23:32.460
is not proper court attire.

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:34.670
If you are looking for
proper court attire,

00:23:34.670 --> 00:23:36.350
I can't recommend INDOCHINO highly enough.

00:23:36.350 --> 00:23:39.870
All of my suits are now
custom made by INDOCHINO.

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:42.400
I buy them with my own money
and I loved them so much that

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:44.070
I reached out personally to them.

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:46.840
And I'm thrilled that they're
now a sponsor of this channel.

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:49.834
The secret to a great
suit is all about fit.

